# Backend (FastAPI)

Owned by house-template-fastapi. Sources: the FastAPI docs (https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/), RFC 9457 (https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457){% if llm == 'langchain' %}, the pydantic-settings docs (https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/), the LangChain and LangSmith docs (https://docs.langchain.com/) and pytest-recording (https://github.com/kiwicom/pytest-recording){% elif llm == 'maf' %}, the pydantic-settings docs (https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/), the Microsoft Agent Framework docs (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/) and pytest-recording (https://github.com/kiwicom/pytest-recording){% else %} and the pydantic-settings docs (https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/){% endif %}.

- The import package is `app` under `backend/src/`; the app is built by `app.main.create_app`. Process-long resources{% if llm != 'none' %} (database engine, model clients){% elif database %} (the database engine){% elif storage %} (the storage client){% endif %} open and close in `lifespan`, never at import time.
- Authentication: protect routes with `app.auth.CurrentUser` (or `OptionalUser`). Replace `app.auth.authenticate` with the project's real verification (session or bearer token, checked against the provider); until then production answers 401 everywhere, and `X-Dev-User` stands in for a user locally and in tests only. `User.id` is the provider's opaque subject, never an email; scope every owned row and query to it.
- API conventions: product routes live in `app/api/` and join `app.api.router` (mounted at `/v1`); a breaking change goes to a new `/v2` router beside it. Each route declares its return type (a Pydantic model{% if database %}, never an ORM object{% endif %}), protects itself with `CurrentUser`, and scopes {% if database %}queries{% else %}data{% endif %} to the user. List endpoints take `PageParamsDep` and return `Page[T]` built with `page()`: keyset (cursor) pagination, never offsets. `app/api/__init__.py` shows a whole resource.
- Dependencies use `Annotated[T, Depends(...)]` aliases. Settings come only from `app.settings.get_settings()` (tests pass a `Settings` to `create_app`); secrets are `SecretStr`, and every new setting goes in `backend/.env.example` with a safe value ({% if llm != 'none' %}model settings: a placeholder{% else %}a placeholder where there is no safe one{% endif %}).
- Errors are RFC 9457 problem details: raise `app.problems.Problem(status, detail=..., headers=...)` for expected failures (`Retry-After` on a 503 or 429).{% if llm != 'none' %} Model provider failures are already mapped (busy 503, timeout 504, input too long 422, unreachable 502, misconfigured 503, with `Retry-After` where retrying helps){% if storage %}; storage raises 400 for a bad key and 404 for a missing file{% endif %}.{% elif storage %} Storage raises 400 for a bad key and 404 for a missing file.{% endif %} Never put submitted values, secrets or stack traces in an error body; unexpected errors are logged with the request id and return a bare 500.
- Every request logs one `request` line (method, route template, status, duration, request id), and library logs (uvicorn{% if database %}, SQLAlchemy{% endif %}{% if llm == 'langchain' %}, LangChain{% elif llm == 'maf' %}, Agent Framework{% endif %}) go through the same renderer, so production logs are JSON throughout. Log with `structlog.get_logger()` and keyword fields (`log.info("course_imported", course_id=...)`), never f-strings, and never log secrets or personal data.
- Readiness checks are registered with `app.health.add_readiness_check`; `/health/live` never touches a dependency.
- Production: {% if database or storage %}the app refuses to start with `APP_ENV=production` on the local default {% if database %}database URL{% if storage %} or storage key{% endif %}{% else %}storage key{% endif %}. {% endif %}{% if database %}Every connection has a statement timeout (30 s) and an idle-in-transaction timeout (60 s); commit or close a session before a long wait{% if llm != 'none' %} (a model call){% endif %}, and never hold a transaction across one. Migrations give up after waiting 5 s for a lock. {% endif %}CORS is off until `CORS_ORIGINS` lists the frontends; set `ALLOWED_HOSTS` in production. Responses carry nosniff, frame-deny, no-referrer and (unless a route sets its own) `Cache-Control: no-store`.
{%- if database %}
- Database: SQLAlchemy 2.1 async with psycopg 3. Inject `app.db.SessionDep`, commit explicitly, load relationships with `selectinload()` and declare them `lazy="raise"`. Models subclass `app.db.Base` and are imported in `app/models.py`.
- Migrations have two branches: the template's (`template`, arriving with template updates) and the project's (`app`). `make migration` writes on `app`; `make migrate` and the migrate job run `alembic upgrade heads`. Never give a project migration a template revision as its parent; use `depends_on` when it needs a template table.
- Every schema change is an Alembic migration (`make migration name="..."`), reviewed by hand: autogenerate misses renames, extensions and some constraints, and a migration says what happens to existing rows. `alembic check` runs in the tests, so a model without a migration fails `make check`. `make check` type-checks `migrations/` too; a plain `uv run mypy` reads only `src` and `tests` (`files` in `pyproject.toml`), so run it through make.
- Database tests use the `db_session` or `db_client` fixtures: a real PostgreSQL with pgvector (testcontainers, or `TEST_DATABASE_URL`), rolled back after each test. Never mock the database.
{%- endif %}
{%- if storage %}
- Files: object storage over the S3 API (`app/storage.py`; R2 in production, RustFS locally with `make storage-up`). Inject `StorageDep`; let browsers upload with `presigned_put(key, content_type=..., size=...)` (type from `STORAGE_UPLOAD_TYPES`, size up to `STORAGE_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES`, both signed) and download with `presigned_get` (always an attachment), so file bodies don't pass through the API. Build keys from ids the server chooses, never from user-supplied names. Storage tests use the `storage_settings` fixture: a real RustFS, a fresh bucket per test. Never mock storage.
{%- endif %}
- Request bodies over `MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` (1 MiB) get a 413; large files must not come through the API.
{%- if llm == 'langchain' %}
- Language models only through the gateway: inject `app.llm.GatewayDep` and call `gateway.model()`; embeddings through `gateway.embeddings()` (`await ...aembed_documents(texts, feature=...)`), under the same budget. Only `app/llm/` may import `langchain` (the gateway is `app/llm/langchain/gateway.py`) or a provider SDK (`lint-imports` enforces it); never build a model elsewhere or pass a `"provider:model"` string to LangChain, because that skips the budget. `langchain_core` (messages, runnables) is fine anywhere. Import from `app.llm.core`, `app.llm.langchain` and `app.llm.GatewayDep`; the model gateway has no other import paths (0.7.0's old ones were removed in 0.8.0), and `mypy` (run by `make check`) reports a leftover; `lint-imports` does not.
- Price arithmetic (`cost`, `ceiling`, `token_details`) and the price lookup live in `app/llm/core/`, which imports no model framework or provider SDK and prices a neutral `Usage` (`from_langchain` in `app/llm/langchain/usage.py` converts LangChain's); only the price tables stay in `app/llm/prices.py`, which is yours to edit. `make check` runs `lint-imports` and `lint-imports --config llm-contracts.toml`; the second keeps the core framework-free. Each failure kind's answer and whether the provider billed it are decided once, in `app/llm/core/errors.py` (`FailureKind`, `OUTCOMES`, `REJECTED`); each adapter classifies its own errors as a kind (`app/llm/langchain/errors.py` for LangChain's `Model*Error` family). New code that calls a provider outside LangChain raises `ProviderError(kind, source=type(error).__name__)` from the provider's error, never with the provider's message (the template's OpenAI embeddings do: they are in `app/llm/core/openai_embeddings.py`, the one core module allowed to import a provider SDK, and `app/llm/core/embeddings.py` meters and batches them on a `Meter`; `GatewayEmbeddings` in `app/llm/langchain/embeddings.py` only turns the `ProviderError` into LangChain's `Model*Error`). The daily budget is in the core too: the `llm_call` ledger and its `Meter` in `app/llm/core/budget.py`, the request bounds in `app/llm/core/bounds.py`, the `llm.*` metrics in `app/llm/core/metrics.py`; LangChain's `BudgetGuard` (`app/llm/langchain/callbacks.py`) only reads LangChain's run and drives the `Meter`. Code that calls a provider outside LangChain drives a `Meter` itself: first `bounds.check_priced(params)`, then size `input_tokens` with `bounds.request_bound` and `output_tokens` with `bounds.output_bound` (`Meter.reserve` checks only the prompt size), then `reserve(call_id, input_tokens=..., output_tokens=..., tags=CallTags(...))` before sending, then `settle(call_id, usage)`, `failed(call_id, kind, streamed=...)` or `abandon(call_id)` if it is cancelled.
- Always await model calls (`ainvoke`, `astream`, `abatch`): the budget can't stop a synchronous `invoke()`. Keep one event loop per process (a worker runs one loop for all its jobs, never `asyncio.run()` per job): the provider integrations cache HTTP connections tied to the first loop. A call the daily budget can't cover is refused with a 503 before it is sent; every call gets an `llm_call` row with its real cost. Send text and images only: the budget can't bound documents, audio or video, so a call carrying them is refused. Calls that would be billed beyond tokens are refused too: a tool the provider runs (web search, file search, code execution) or a dearer service tier (priority); give the model a tool the app runs instead, or add those prices first.
- Personal data: unless `LLM_REDACTION=off`, `gateway.model()` is a `RedactingChatModel` that replaces contact, bank and ID numbers in common formats, and the values you name, with placeholders such as `[EMAIL_1]` before a call leaves for the provider, and puts the originals back in the answer, tool-call arguments, structured fields and streamed text (decision 0006; the code is `app/llm/langchain/redaction.py`, the core in `app/llm/core/redaction.py`, with the framework-free string walkers and the restorer of the originals in `app/llm/core/redaction_data.py`). Tool definitions and output schemas are scanned too (descriptions, titles, property names, allowed and example values); names, types, formats and `$ref`s are identifiers the provider checks and are left alone, a Pydantic schema is sent as a subclass carrying the redacted schema and the answer comes back as your own class, and a schema rule that holds personal data (`pattern`) stops matching what it meant. Name what the app knows around the call: `async with redaction_scope(known={"NAME": [user.full_name]}, allow=[the property's address]): ...` (a context variable, so concurrent requests do not mix); add detectors in `app/llm/redaction_rules.py`, each linear time (a project detector's time is not checked yet, #94) and tried on your own property text. Never put personal data in call metadata, tags, `feature` or `user_ref`: metadata goes to LangSmith and to logs. A placeholder shown to a user is a bug (`llm_unrestored_placeholders` counts them); a failure to redact is a 503 and nothing is sent. It is not complete (names you do not pass, free-form addresses, special-category text, text inside images; #97) and not anonymisation. Text sent for embeddings is cleaned the same way, one way and each text on its own (nothing is restored): embed documents and their queries under the same `redaction_scope`, re-embed what you stored before, look exact identifiers up in your own columns, and pass `redactor=gateway.redactor` if you build `GatewayEmbeddings` yourself. Overriding `GatewayEmbeddings._embed` or building LangChain's own embeddings client (`init_embeddings`, `OpenAIEmbeddings`, `AzureOpenAIEmbeddings`) skips both the budget and the redaction; a test catches the usual ways, and it is a guard, not a proof. `isinstance(gateway.model(), ChatOpenAI)` is False; thinking blocks pass unchanged; a structured answer that does not parse into its schema (`with_structured_output`, an agent's `response_format`) is a 502 "The language model's answer didn't match the expected format.", settled at its cost, with the parser's message (it quotes the answer) left out, and a schema validator that rejects a placeholder (EmailStr, a pattern) is one; with `LLM_REDACTION=off`, `gateway.model()` is the provider's model and a pydantic `ValidationError` from its parser is not turned into the 502; the caller's own `LangChainTracer` with its own client records what that client is given; the gateway's `langsmith.configure(client=...)` replaces any process-wide LangSmith client the app set itself (its hiding or anonymizer is lost); a message `name` that holds personal data is refused, because no placeholder fits there.
- Reservations a crashed process left open count against the day at their reserved cost; `make llm-report` reports any older than 30 minutes, and `python -m app.llm.core.report --expire-stale` (or a scheduled job calling `expire_stale`) closes them. Prompts past a price's `max_prompt_tokens` (OpenAI: 272K) are refused with a 422 rather than under-recorded. An embedding price's `max_prompt_tokens` applies to each request: texts are batched under it, and one text past it alone is refused with a 422.
- Tag model calls with what they're for and whom for: `config=gateway.call_config(user, "answer", prompt)`. With `LLM_USER_DAILY_BUDGET_USD` set, each user's calls are capped per day (429 when used up) as well as the global budget (503); set it whenever users can trigger model calls. `feature` is a short fixed name; `user_ref` an opaque id, never a name or email. Every call's tokens (reasoning and cache included), cost and latency land in `llm_call`; `make llm-report` sums them by day, model, prompt or feature, and a log warns once a day at 80% of the budget.
- Observability: FastAPI's native OpenTelemetry traces requests (not `/health`), records HTTP metrics and logs unhandled exceptions; the gateway adds `llm.calls`, `llm.tokens`, `llm.cost` and `llm.latency`. Nothing is exported until the `OTEL_*` settings in `.env.example` are set. Keep metric labels to bounded sets (no user ids).
- The template sets no model: `LLM_MODEL` and `EMBEDDING_MODEL` (`<provider>:<model-id>`) must be set, and the gateway refuses to start without them or with one that has no price. A model needs its prices in `app/llm/prices.py` (embedding models in `EMBEDDING_PRICES`) before a setting names it: integer micro-USD per million tokens, read from the provider's pricing page that day, with `checked` (ISO date) and `source` (the page's URL) on the entry; an entry without both fails validation. Never type a price from memory, and don't write a literal model id in docs or `.env.example`.
- Prompts are Markdown files in `app/prompts/`, loaded with `load_prompt(name)` (`app/llm/langchain/prompts.py`) and sent with `config=prompt.config()` so each call records the prompt's version. Never fetch prompts at runtime. The prompt, its hash and `prompt_text` are in `app/llm/core/prompts.py` (no model framework); `prompt_config` and `config()` are LangChain's.
- An agent makes at most `AGENT_MAX_MODEL_CALLS` model calls per run (default 13). The last call is made without tools, so the model writes a final answer, and the result carries `step_limit_reached` (`app.llm.langchain.steps.step_limit_reached(result)`): show users when an answer was cut short. Derive or check every `thread_id` server-side against the current user (e.g. `f"{user.id}:{conversation_id}"` after confirming they own the conversation): a caller-supplied thread id would let one user read another's thread.
- Agents: `gateway.agent(tools, system_prompt=...)` builds LangChain's `create_agent` on the gateway's model with the Postgres checkpointer; pass a `thread_id` in `configurable`. For more than a plain tool loop, build a LangGraph `StateGraph` whose nodes call `gateway.model()` and compile it with `checkpointer=gateway.checkpointer`. Checkpoints load strictly: a saved object is rebuilt only if its class is on LangGraph's safe list (messages, dates, UUIDs, `Send` and so on) or in `ALLOWED` in `app/llm/checkpoint_types.py` (yours to edit); any other class comes back as its plain data (a dict, a list, or an enum's value) and LangGraph logs `Blocked deserialization` once per class. Unlike `LANGGRAPH_STRICT_MSGPACK`, which derives an allowlist from the graph's state types in `compile`, this does not: list every class your state keeps (pydantic models, dataclasses, enums, a `response_format` result, tool artifacts) before you deploy. List only plain data classes whose construction has no side effects. A test fails on any `create_agent`/`init_chat_model` given a model string. The checkpoint tables come from Alembic (`app/llm/langchain/checkpoints.py`); when a langgraph-checkpoint-postgres upgrade adds a migration, a test says so: add a revision that calls `checkpoints.apply_migrations` inside `autocommit_block()`, importing it from `app.llm.langchain` (the old path no longer exists).
- Evals live in `tests/evals/`, marked `eval` and `langsmith`, and run only with `make eval` (capped budget; uploaded to LangSmith when `LANGSMITH_API_KEY` is set). `test_gateway_evals.py` checks the gateway itself against the real provider (real cost settled, streamed usage, structured output, an agent's tool loop, the budget refusing); put the product's evals in their own files beside it. Log inputs, outputs, reference outputs and feedback with `langsmith.testing`; assert only what must hold for a release.
- Keys for `make record-llm` and `make eval` come from the shell or `backend/.env` (git-ignored; never commit a key or paste one into a command), and `make eval` also needs your `LLM_MODEL` and `EMBEDDING_MODEL` there or in the shell (`make record-llm` uses the models pinned in `tests/models.py`; `LLM_EVAL` is for `make eval` only and `make check` unsets it). The tests themselves never read `backend/.env`.
- Tests never call a model: mark a test that does `@pytest.mark.vcr` and `@pytest.mark.block_network`, and it replays `tests/cassettes/`. Record with `make record-llm` (real key, capped spend; it records with the models pinned in `tests/models.py`, so to record with another model change them there first), check the cassette holds no secrets or personal data, and commit it. Test budget logic with LangChain's fake chat models.
{%- elif llm == 'maf' %}
- Language models only through the gateway: inject `app.llm.GatewayDep` and call `gateway.client()`, the Microsoft Agent Framework (MAF) chat client for `LLM_MODEL`'s provider (OpenAI Responses or Anthropic), built by us with the SDK's timeout and retries (`app/llm/maf/gateway.py`); embeddings through `gateway.embeddings()` (`await ...embed_documents(texts, feature=...)`), under the same budget. Only `app/llm/maf/` may import `agent_framework` (and its provider packages) and only `app/llm/` a provider SDK (`lint-imports` and a test enforce it); never build a MAF client elsewhere or hand a model string to MAF, because that skips the budget and the redaction. `gateway.client()` refuses a client whose middleware chain is not `[RedactionMiddleware, BudgetMiddleware]` in that order. Import from `app.llm.core`, `app.llm.maf` and `app.llm.GatewayDep`.
- Tag a call with what it is for and whom for: `with gateway.call_options(user, "answer", prompt): response = await gateway.client().get_response(messages)`. The tags travel in context variables (MAF has no verified per-call metadata path), so they follow the call into the tool loop. `feature` is a short fixed name; `user_ref` an opaque id, never a name or email. With `LLM_USER_DAILY_BUDGET_USD` set, each user's calls are capped per day (429 when used up) as well as the global budget (503). Every call's tokens (reasoning and cache included), cost and latency land in `llm_call`; `make llm-report` sums them.
- The middleware does the rest: it sends `store=False` to OpenAI's Responses API on every call (which stores by default; Anthropic's API stores nothing and takes no such option) and refuses `store=True`: history stays in our database, not at the provider, sets `max_tokens` from `LLM_MAX_TOKENS` unless the call asks for more (then the reservation covers the larger one), refuses a request billed beyond tokens (a tool the provider runs, a dearer service tier) and documents, audio or video, reserves the most the call can cost before it is sent, and settles it from the provider's usage (`usage_details` as the neutral `Usage` in `app/llm/maf/usage.py`). A call the daily budget can't cover is refused with a 503 before it is sent. A streamed call (`get_response(..., stream=True)`) is reserved when it is first read and settled from the usage the provider reports at its end; read it with `async with stream:` (MAF's documented way to stop early), though one dropped without closing is still settled when it is collected. A stream stopped early, or failing after text arrived, keeps its reservation (the provider may bill what it generated and reports nothing); a cancelled one stays reserved until `--expire-stale`. MAF's update merge rule applies to what you read: `merge_mode="append"` text is a piece, `"replace"` text is the whole text so far (OpenAI's `output_text.done`). With `response_format`, the answer must parse into it (`response.value`, or `get_final_response().value` for a stream): one that does not, or has no value (a refusal), is a 502 "The language model's answer didn't match the expected format.", the call settled at its cost (`SchemaMismatch` in `app/llm/core/errors.py`). The SDK client is the only retry layer (MAF's Anthropic client never retries); the gateway closes it at shutdown.
- Agents: `gateway.agent(tools, instructions=prompt.text)` is MAF's `Agent` on the gateway's client, so every model call of its tool loop is redacted and budgeted; run it inside `gateway.call_options(...)` so the calls carry their tags. A run makes at most `AGENT_MAX_MODEL_CALLS` model calls (default 13): the last is made with `tool_choice="none"`, so the model writes a final answer, and `app.llm.maf.steps.step_limit_reached(response)` is true (logged once as `step_limit_reached`): tell users the answer was cut short. The count restarts on every run; our own counter (`StepRun`, put in each run's options by the gateway's client and removed by the budget middleware before the provider) decides, and MAF's `max_iterations` (N - 1) is the backstop. The gateway does not store threads yet: keep the `AgentSession` you pass to `run` yourself.
- Never add chat middleware per call (`client_kwargs={"middleware": ...}`) or per agent (`Agent(..., middleware=[...])`): MAF would run it after redaction and the budget, so it could add personal data or spend unmetered, and the gateway's clients refuse it with `RedactionUnavailable`; add it to the gateway's own chain instead (function middleware around tools is not affected). Options a call may carry are a fixed list (`ALLOWED_OPTIONS` in `app/llm/maf/middleware.py`): `extra_body`, `conversation_id`, `previous_response_id`, `user`, `metadata` and anything unknown are refused; `reasoning`, `verbosity` and `thinking` pass (a thinking budget must be under `max_tokens`). The reservation counts every tool definition and output schema in full, and `AGENT_MAX_MODEL_CALLS` caps a tool loop at exactly that many model calls: MAF makes its last call with `tool_choice="none"` (model calls = `max_iterations` + 1), and the gateway's own counter sends that last call with `tool_choice="none"` too, so at a limit of 1 no tools are offered. MAF's compaction would write summaries of the redacted messages into a thread: it is not used yet (#46).
- Failures are classified by walking the error's `__cause__`, `__context__`, exception-group members and `args` (MAF wraps the provider's error: an Anthropic 429 arrives as an invalid-request error) and answered by `app/llm/core/errors.py` without the provider's words; `retry-after-ms` and `retry-after` are read from the provider's response. MAF's own error text can hold the prompt: never log or return it.
- Personal data: unless `LLM_REDACTION=off`, `RedactionMiddleware` replaces contact, bank and ID numbers in common formats, and the values you name, with placeholders such as `[EMAIL_1]` before a call leaves for the provider, and puts the originals back in the answer, streamed text included (a placeholder split across updates is held back until whole; a tool call's arguments arrive empty and then whole at the end of its model call) (decision 0006; the code is `app/llm/maf/redaction.py`, the core in `app/llm/core/redaction.py` and `app/llm/core/redaction_data.py`). Tool definitions and output schemas are scanned too (descriptions, titles, property names, allowed and example values); names, types, formats and `$ref`s are identifiers the provider checks and are left alone, a Pydantic schema is sent as a subclass carrying the redacted schema and the answer comes back as your own class, and a schema rule that holds personal data (`pattern`) stops matching what it meant. Name what the app knows around the call: `async with redaction_scope(known={"NAME": [user.full_name]}, allow=[the property's address]): ...`. Never put personal data in call tags, `feature` or `user_ref`. A failure to redact (or a content type the walkers do not know) is a 503 and nothing is sent; images are not scanned (logged as `llm_unscanned_images`). It is not complete and not anonymisation. Text sent for embeddings is cleaned the same way, one way: embed documents and their queries under the same `redaction_scope`.
- Reservations a crashed process left open count against the day at their reserved cost; `make llm-report` reports any older than 30 minutes, and `python -m app.llm.core.report --expire-stale` closes them. Prompts past a price's `max_prompt_tokens` are refused with a 422 rather than under-recorded.
- The template sets no model: `LLM_MODEL` and `EMBEDDING_MODEL` (`<provider>:<model-id>`) must be set, and the gateway refuses to start without them or with one that has no price in `app/llm/prices.py` (integer micro-USD per million tokens, read from the provider's pricing page that day, with `checked` and `source`). Never type a price from memory, and don't write a literal model id in docs or `.env.example`. An Anthropic cache write is reported without its lifetime and priced at the longer lifetime's rate: a deliberate over-count.
- Prompts are Markdown files in `app/prompts/`, loaded with `load_prompt(name)` (`app/llm/maf/prompts.py`) and passed to `call_options` so each call records the prompt's version. Never fetch prompts at runtime.
- Switching `llm` from `langchain` to `maf` by `copier update` is supported; from `maf` back to `langchain` is not yet (one-way): migration 0003 (LangGraph's checkpoint tables) is empty under `maf`, so a project that never ran LangChain has no such tables and the `alembic stamp 0001` recipe cannot add them. To go back, render a fresh `llm: langchain` project and move your data.
- `ENABLE_SENSITIVE_DATA` (MAF's setting that puts prompts and answers in telemetry spans) must stay off while redaction is on: spans would hold the redacted text at best and the originals at worst. The app does not yet refuse to start with it on (planned with tracing, #35 part 7); never set it.
- The MAF packages (`agent-framework-core`, `-openai`, `-anthropic` and `msgspec`) are pinned with `==` in the `maf` extra and move together, reviewed by hand: the Anthropic one is a beta. Dependabot never merges them unreviewed; read the release notes of each (a `[BREAKING]` change is marked there) and run `make check` before moving any pin.
- Tests never call a model: mark a test that does `@pytest.mark.vcr` and `@pytest.mark.block_network`, and it replays `tests/cassettes/`. Record with `make record-llm` (real key, capped spend; it records with the models pinned in `tests/models.py`), check the cassette holds no secrets or personal data, and commit it. The shared tests (`tests/test_llm_parity_*.py`) run through `tests/harness/maf.py`: a scripted MAF client composed with MAF's real layers and the gateway's own middleware.
{%- endif %}
- Tests are async (`pytestmark = pytest.mark.anyio`) and drive the app in-process through the `client` fixture; a bug fix comes with a test that fails without it.
- The image (`backend/Dockerfile`, `make image`; `make up` runs it{% if database %} with the database{% endif %}) installs the app from the lockfile into a virtualenv and runs it as UID 10001 with uvicorn, one process per container. On SIGTERM it finishes requests for `UVICORN_TIMEOUT_GRACEFUL_SHUTDOWN` (25 s): keep that under the platform's grace period. Set `FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` to the load balancer's addresses so client IPs and the scheme are right.{% if database %} Migrations never run at start-up: the `migrate` service locally, and a one-off job with the same image and `alembic upgrade heads` before each rollout.{% endif %} Deploy by the digest the publish workflow outputs, never a tag. Keep secrets out of the image (`.dockerignore`) and pass them as environment variables.
- Dependencies: add to `backend/pyproject.toml`, run `make lock`, commit both files. Optional parts are extras (`[project.optional-dependencies]`: `db` comes with the `database` answer, `langchain` with `llm: langchain`, `maf` with `llm: maf`, `storage` with the `storage` answer); `make install`, the make targets and the image install the ones this project answered for (`EXTRAS` in `mk/fastapi.mk`, `--extra` in the Dockerfile). To switch a part, commit first, then run `copier update --trust --defaults -a .copier-answers.fastapi.yml {% if database %}-d database=false` to turn the database off{% if llm != 'none' %} (the model gateway needs it, so it goes too){% endif %}, {% if llm != 'none' %}`-d llm=none` to turn only the model gateway off{% else %}`-d llm=langchain` to turn the model gateway on{% endif %}, or `-d storage={{ 'false' if storage else 'true' }}` to turn storage {{ 'off' if storage else 'on' }}{% else %}-d database=true` to turn the database on (the gateway stays off once the answers file records `llm`, which the first 0.8.0 update does) (add `-d llm=langchain` for the model gateway too: it needs the database), or `-d storage={{ 'false' if storage else 'true' }}` to turn storage {{ 'off' if storage else 'on' }}{% endif %}, then `make lock && make install && make check`.{% if database %} Turning the database off deletes the template's database {% if llm != 'none' %}and model-gateway {% endif %}files even if you edited them (`app/db.py`, `app/models.py`, {% if llm != 'none' %}`app/llm/`, `app/prompts/`, {% endif %}`migrations/`, `compose/db/compose.yaml`, `tests/db.py`, the database {% if llm != 'none' %}and model {% endif %}tests; recover edits from git); your own files and `backend/uv.lock` stay. Tables are never dropped: to drop them, run `uv --directory backend run --locked --extra db{% if llm != 'none' %} --extra {{ llm }}{% endif %} alembic downgrade base` before updating, while Alembic and the migrations still exist. Your own migrations in `migrations/versions/` are left behind; `make check` still lints them with ruff but no longer type-checks them (Alembic is not installed): delete or move them. Your own code that imports `app.db`{% if llm != 'none' %}, `app.models` or `app.llm`{% else %} or `app.models`{% endif %} then fails `mypy`, and your own tests that use `db_session` or `db_client` fail in pytest. Remove `DATABASE_URL`{% if llm != 'none' %} and the model settings{% endif %} from `backend/.env`; a running database container and the `db-data` volume are left behind (`docker compose down --remove-orphans`, then remove the volume).{% endif %}{% if llm != 'none' %} Turning the model gateway off (`-d llm=none`) deletes its files even if you edited them (`app/llm/`, including your `prices.py`, {% if llm == 'langchain' %}`checkpoint_types.py` {% endif %}and `redaction_rules.py`, `app/prompts/`, `llm-contracts.toml`, `tests/models.py`, `tests/recording.py`, the model tests, evals and cassettes; recover edits from git) and keeps the database; `app/models.py`, `migrations/env.py` and migrations 0002 to 0005 change (expect conflicts where you edited them; `app/models.py` very likely has your imports of the gateway's tables). Tables are never dropped, and the four migrations then do nothing: to drop the gateway's tables, run `uv --directory backend run --locked --extra db --extra {{ llm }} alembic downgrade 0001` before updating (this also downgrades any of your own migrations that depend on a gateway revision). Your own code that imports `app.llm` then fails `mypy`. Remove `LLM_*`, `EMBEDDING_MODEL`, `*_API_KEY`, `AGENT_*` and `LANGSMITH_*` from `backend/.env` (leftovers are ignored); `make migration` ignores the tables left behind.{% endif %}{% if database and llm == 'none' %} A database that ran without the model tables (migrations 0002 to 0006 did nothing there) gets them only after `copier update` has turned the model gateway on (stamping first would make `make migrate` re-run the empty revisions and leave no tables). Then, on every database the project runs against, production and staging included, and before the gateway is deployed, run `uv --directory backend run --locked --extra db --extra langchain alembic stamp 0001`, then `make migrate`; a new database needs only `make migrate`.{% endif %}{% if storage %} Turning storage off deletes the template's storage files even if you edited them (`app/storage.py`, `tests/storage.py`, `tests/test_storage.py`, `compose/storage/compose.yaml`; recover edits from git); your own files and `backend/uv.lock` stay. Your own tests that use the storage test fixtures then fail in pytest, and a running storage container and the `storage-data` volume are left behind (`docker compose down --remove-orphans`, then remove the volume).{% endif %} FastAPI stays pinned to one minor (`<0.N+1`) because 0.x minors can break.
